From: Kali NetHunter rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] nfc: s3fwrn5: declare NFC-DEP so the chip can enter listen mode

The s3fwrn5 device is allocated with six poll/reader protocols and no target
protocol, so `nfc_genl_start_poll` rejects any `tm_protocols` the client asks
for and the controller never enters listen mode. The whole listen path in the
NCI core -- `nci_set_listen_parameters()`, the `NCI_NFC_A_PASSIVE_LISTEN_MODE`
config in `nci_rf_discover_req()`, the `NCI_LISTEN_ACTIVE` state machine -- is
already there, but it is gated on `NFC_PROTO_NFC_DEP_MASK`, which this driver
does not advertise.

Add it. With NFC-DEP declared, a START_POLL carrying
`tm_protocols = NFC_PROTO_NFC_DEP_MASK` makes the core send a RF_DISCOVER with
NFC-A/F passive listen, and the S3NRN4V does the NFC-A anticollision in
hardware -- so an external reader sees a card. That is the first step toward
card emulation on this chip, which Android drives on the same silicon.

This is the minimum to prove listen mode works on the hardware. Presenting a
specific tag (fixed UID, Type 2/Type 4 responses) is follow-up work in the NCI
core, tracked in docs/nfc.md.

--- a/drivers/nfc/s3fwrn5/core.c
+++ b/drivers/nfc/s3fwrn5/core.c
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 #define S3FWRN5_NFC_PROTOCOLS  (NFC_PROTO_JEWEL_MASK | \
 				NFC_PROTO_MIFARE_MASK | \
 				NFC_PROTO_FELICA_MASK | \
+				NFC_PROTO_NFC_DEP_MASK | \
 				NFC_PROTO_ISO14443_MASK | \
 				NFC_PROTO_ISO14443_B_MASK | \
 				NFC_PROTO_ISO15693_MASK)
